  "image_name": "f738391dbdc06faa789bf1874ebb9114.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three major ways a droplet’s shape and wetting can be altered at a surface. On the left, the key idea is interfacial energy balance at the contact line: the contact angle depends on the tensions between liquid, solid, and vapor. A schematic below contrasts a surface that “adapts” near the contact line, where the solid-related energies vary over a short length, with one showing no adaptation, where the interfacial energies stay uniform across the wetted region.\n\nThe middle section illustrates electrostatic effects, apparently electrowetting. Charges and electric-field lines are shown between a droplet and an inclined, grounded substrate, with zoom-ins suggesting that ions gather near the contact line and that an electric field acts across a thin dielectric layer beneath the liquid. The overall message is that applied voltage changes the effective surface energetics and thus the droplet shape and motion.\n\nOn the right, the figure shows soft surfaces. Instead of a rigid contact line, the substrate deforms into a small wetting ridge under the droplet. The apparent contact angle is compared with a local angle near the deformed edge, emphasizing that on elastomers or other compliant materials, surface deformation modifies the observed wetting geometry.",
